--- !u!114 &100504129323994776
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'In HDRP, Volumes allow you to overrides HDRP effects on your camera. This includes post-processing effects, shadows, lighting, Fog and Sky settings, among others.


      Volumes can be set to <b>Global</b> or <b>Local</b> mode.


      <b>Global</b> volume overrides are applied to all cameras in the Scene regardless of their position. A <b>Global</b> volume can be considered as infinitely large and is useful to control settings affecting the entire world, such as Sky and Fog.


      <b>Local</b> volumes overrides are applied only to cameras whose position is within the zone of influence defined by the <b>Local</b> Volumes. The local volumes can be a box, a sphere, as well as a custom (convex) mesh.'
